-- Q1: hard / L4 — Major fund determination criteria (10%/5% rule)
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$Riverdale County has a special revenue fund with total assets of $18 million, total liabilities of $6 million, and total revenues of $9 million. The totals for all governmental funds are $150 million in assets, $50 million in liabilities, and $80 million in revenues. The totals for all governmental and enterprise funds combined are $250 million in assets, $90 million in liabilities, and $140 million in revenues. Evaluate whether this fund qualifies as a major fund under GASB 34's quantitative criteria.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It qualifies because its assets meet both the 10% governmental-category test and the 5% combined-total test", "It does not qualify because it fails the 10% threshold for all elements", "It qualifies solely because its revenues exceed 5% of combined totals", "It does not qualify because the 5% combined-total test is not met for any element"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): GASB 34 para. 76 requires a fund to be at least 10% of the corresponding total for its category (governmental or enterprise) AND at least 5% of the combined governmental-plus-enterprise total for the same element. Assets: $18M ÷ $150M governmental = 12% (passes 10%), and $18M ÷ $250M combined = 7.2% (passes 5%). Both thresholds are met for assets, so the fund is major. Wrong (B): Assets clearly exceed 10% of governmental totals (12%), so the fund does pass for at least one element. Wrong (C): The fund qualifies on assets, not solely on revenues; revenues are $9M ÷ $80M = 11.25% of governmental but only 6.4% of combined — also qualifying, but assets pass as well. Wrong (D): Assets at 7.2% of combined totals exceed the 5% threshold.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Compensated absences fund vs government-wide
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$Maplewood City estimates its total compensated absences liability at $3.2 million as of fiscal year-end. Of this amount, $400,000 is expected to be paid with currently available resources within the next fiscal year. Under GASB standards, how should Maplewood report this liability in its governmental fund financial statements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Report the full $3.2 million as a fund liability", "Report only $400,000 as a fund liability", "Report $3.2 million as a deferred inflow of resources", "Report no liability because compensated absences are never recognized in governmental funds"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): GASB Codification Section C60 provides that governmental funds recognize compensated absences only to the extent they will be liquidated with expendable available financial resources. The $400,000 expected to be paid from current resources is recognized as a fund liability; the remaining $2.8 million is reported only in the government-wide statements. Wrong (A): Governmental funds use the current financial resources measurement focus and do not report the full long-term liability. Wrong (C): Compensated absences are liabilities, not deferred inflows of resources. Wrong (D): The currently due portion is recognized in governmental funds when it will be paid with available resources.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Net pension liability calculation
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$Oakridge Township participates in a single-employer defined benefit pension plan. The plan's total pension liability is $85 million, and the fair value of plan net position (plan assets) is $62 million. Under GASB 68, what is the net pension liability reported on Oakridge's government-wide statement of net position?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$62 million", "$85 million", "$23 million", "$147 million"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): GASB 68 para. 29 defines the net pension liability as the total pension liability minus the plan net position (fair value of plan assets). $85 million − $62 million = $23 million. This amount is reported on the government-wide statement of net position. Wrong (A): $62 million represents plan assets, not the net pension liability. Wrong (B): $85 million is the total pension liability before subtracting plan assets. Wrong (D): $147 million incorrectly adds the two figures instead of subtracting.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Nonspendable fund balance definition
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$Under GASB 54, which category of fund balance includes amounts that are not in a spendable form, such as inventories and prepaid items?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Restricted", "Committed", "Assigned", "Nonspendable"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): GASB 54 para. 6 defines nonspendable fund balance as amounts that cannot be spent because they are either not in spendable form (e.g., inventories, prepaid items, long-term receivables) or are legally or contractually required to be maintained intact. Wrong (A): Restricted fund balance has externally imposed constraints from creditors, grantors, or laws. Wrong (B): Committed fund balance requires formal action by the government's highest decision-making authority. Wrong (C): Assigned fund balance reflects intended uses established by the governing body or a designated official.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: easy / L2 — Private-purpose trust fund vs permanent fund
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$A wealthy donor establishes a trust with the City of Grandview, stipulating that both principal and earnings must be used to award college scholarships to private citizens. Under GASB standards, this trust should be reported in which fund type?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A private-purpose trust fund", "A permanent fund", "A special revenue fund", "The General Fund"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): GASB 34 para. 70 requires that trust arrangements where the government holds resources for the benefit of individuals, private organizations, or other governments be reported in private-purpose trust funds (a fiduciary fund type). The key distinction is that the beneficiaries are private citizens, not the government itself. Wrong (B): Permanent funds account for resources where the principal must remain intact and earnings benefit the government or its citizenry as a governmental program, not specific private individuals. Wrong (C): Special revenue funds account for restricted revenue sources for specific governmental purposes, not trust arrangements for private beneficiaries. Wrong (D): The General Fund is for general operations and would not separately track trust resources.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Budgetary journal entry mechanics
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$The City of Havenbrook adopts its annual budget for the General Fund, estimating revenues of $18 million and authorizing appropriations of $17.2 million. Apply GASB budgetary accounting standards to determine the correct journal entry to record this adopted budget.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Debit Appropriations $17.2M, Credit Estimated Revenues $18M, Credit Budgetary Fund Balance $0.8M", "Debit Estimated Revenues $18M, Credit Appropriations $17.2M, Credit Budgetary Fund Balance $0.8M", "Debit Cash $18M, Credit Appropriations $17.2M, Credit Unassigned Fund Balance $0.8M", "Debit Estimated Revenues $18M, Debit Budgetary Fund Balance $0.8M, Credit Appropriations $17.2M"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB budgetary accounting, the adopted budget is recorded by debiting Estimated Revenues (an anticipated resource inflow) for $18M and crediting Appropriations (authorized spending authority) for $17.2M. Since estimated revenues exceed appropriations, the $0.8M difference is credited to Budgetary Fund Balance, reflecting a projected surplus. Wrong (A): This reverses debits and credits; Appropriations is a credit (spending authority), not a debit. Wrong (C): Cash is not debited when recording the budget — no cash has been received; the entry records estimates only. Wrong (D): Budgetary Fund Balance should be credited (not debited) when revenues exceed appropriations, reflecting a projected surplus.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Allowance for uncollectible taxes and revenue recognition interaction
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$The Town of Silverton levies $10 million in property taxes for its fiscal year ending December 31. Historical data suggests 3% will be uncollectible. By December 31, $8.5 million is collected. During January and February (within 60 days), $1 million is collected. The remaining $500,000 is expected to be collected in April through June. Evaluate the revenue recognized, deferred inflows, and allowance for uncollectible taxes in Silverton's governmental fund statements.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Revenue $9.5M, deferred inflows $200K, allowance $300K", "Revenue $8.5M, deferred inflows $1.2M, allowance $300K", "Revenue $9.5M, deferred inflows $0, allowance $500K", "Revenue $9.2M, deferred inflows $500K, allowance $300K"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under modified accrual, property tax revenue is recognized when measurable and available (GASB Codification P70). Collected by year-end ($8.5M) plus collected within 60 days ($1.0M) = $9.5M available revenue. Allowance for uncollectible taxes is 3% of $10M = $300K. Remaining collectible amount beyond the availability period: $500K − $300K uncollectible = $200K reported as deferred inflows of resources. Wrong (B): Understates revenue by excluding the $1M collected within the 60-day availability period. Wrong (C): Incorrectly shows $0 deferred inflows and overstates the allowance — the $200K collectible beyond 60 days must be deferred, and uncollectible is $300K not $500K. Wrong (D): Incorrectly reduces revenue by the deferred amount and misstates the deferred inflows.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: easy / L2 — Investment trust fund purpose
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$Whitfield County operates an investment pool in which several smaller school districts and townships invest their idle cash. Under GASB standards, the external participants' share of the investment pool is reported in which fund type?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The General Fund", "An enterprise fund", "A special revenue fund", "An investment trust fund"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): GASB 34 para. 70 requires that external investment pools — where a government invests on behalf of other legally separate entities — be reported in investment trust funds, a fiduciary fund type. The external participants' equity is the portion reported in this fund. Wrong (A): The General Fund is for general government operations and does not separately track fiduciary resources held for external participants. Wrong (B): Enterprise funds account for business-type activities that charge fees to external customers, not investment pool management for other governments. Wrong (C): Special revenue funds account for legally restricted governmental revenue sources, not fiduciary investment activities.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q9: hard / L3 — Pension expense components under GASB 68
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$Ridgewood City reports the following pension-related data for its single-employer plan: service cost $2.4 million, interest on total pension liability $5.1 million, expected return on plan assets $3.8 million, amortization of deferred outflows from assumption changes $600,000, and amortization of deferred inflows from investment experience $400,000. Apply GASB 68 to determine the pension expense reported on the government-wide statement of activities.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$3.9 million", "$5.7 million", "$4.7 million", "$8.1 million"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB 68 para. 32-36, pension expense includes service cost ($2.4M) + interest on TPL ($5.1M) − expected return on plan assets ($3.8M) + amortization of deferred outflows ($0.6M) − amortization of deferred inflows ($0.4M) = $3.9M. Deferred inflow amortization reduces pension expense. Wrong (B): This incorrectly adds rather than subtracts the deferred inflow amortization ($3.9M + $0.4M + $1.4M is not correct). Wrong (C): This omits the deferred inflow amortization from the calculation. Wrong (D): This incorrectly adds the expected return on plan assets instead of subtracting it.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — General revenues on statement of activities
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$On the government-wide statement of activities, what term describes revenues such as property taxes and unrestricted state aid that are not tied to a specific program or function?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Program revenues", "General revenues", "Special revenues", "Derived revenues"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): GASB 34 para. 37 defines general revenues as all revenues that are not required to be reported as program revenues. Property taxes, unrestricted grants, and investment earnings are typical general revenues reported at the bottom of the statement of activities, separate from the net cost format of program expenses and revenues. Wrong (A): Program revenues are directly associated with a specific function or program (charges for services, operating grants, capital grants). Wrong (C): Special revenues is a fund classification (special revenue funds), not a revenue classification on the statement of activities. Wrong (D): Derived revenues is a category under GASB 33's nonexchange transaction classification, not a presentation category on the statement of activities.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Operating grants vs capital grants classification
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$The City of Eastwood receives a $2 million state grant restricted to purchasing new police vehicles and a $500,000 federal grant restricted to funding after-school tutoring programs. On the government-wide statement of activities, how should these grants be classified as program revenues?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both are reported as operating grants and contributions", "Both are reported as capital grants and contributions", "The $2 million is a capital grant and the $500,000 is an operating grant", "Both are reported as general revenues because they are intergovernmental"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 34 para. 48-52, program revenues include operating grants (restricted to operating purposes) and capital grants (restricted to capital acquisitions). The $2M grant for police vehicles is a capital grant because it funds capital asset purchases. The $500K for tutoring programs is an operating grant because it supports program operations. Wrong (A): The vehicle grant funds capital acquisitions, not operations, so it is a capital grant. Wrong (B): The tutoring grant funds ongoing program operations, not capital assets, so it is an operating grant. Wrong (D): Restricted grants tied to specific programs are program revenues, not general revenues, regardless of being intergovernmental.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Committed fund balance
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$Under GASB 54, which level of fund balance constraint requires formal action by a government's highest level of decision-making authority, such as a city council resolution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Restricted", "Assigned", "Nonspendable", "Committed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): GASB 54 para. 10 defines committed fund balance as amounts constrained for specific purposes by formal action of the government's highest level of decision-making authority (e.g., a city council ordinance or resolution). Modification or removal of the constraint also requires formal action at the same level. Wrong (A): Restricted fund balance has externally enforceable constraints imposed by creditors, grantors, contributors, or laws of other governments. Wrong (B): Assigned fund balance reflects the government's intended use, established by the governing body or a body or official delegated that authority — it does not require formal action. Wrong (C): Nonspendable fund balance is based on the form of the resource (not in spendable form) or legal requirements, not on governing body action.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — GASB 68 vs ASC 715 pension comparison
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$A CPA is comparing pension accounting standards for a state government client and a private-sector corporate client. Apply GASB 68 and ASC 715 to determine how the primary pension liability reported on the balance sheet differs between these two frameworks.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["GASB 68 reports the net pension liability (TPL minus plan assets) on the statement of net position, while ASC 715 reports the projected benefit obligation net of plan assets on the balance sheet", "GASB 68 reports only the unfunded accumulated benefit obligation, while ASC 715 reports the full projected benefit obligation", "GASB 68 discloses the pension liability only in footnotes, while ASC 715 requires on-face reporting", "GASB 68 uses the accumulated benefit obligation, while ASC 715 uses the total pension liability"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): GASB 68 requires governments to report the net pension liability (total pension liability minus plan net position at fair value) on the face of the government-wide statement of net position. ASC 715 requires private companies to report the funded status of the plan (projected benefit obligation minus fair value of plan assets) on the balance sheet. Both report a net liability, but the terminology and certain measurement details differ. Wrong (B): GASB 68 uses the total pension liability, not the accumulated benefit obligation. Wrong (C): GASB 68 requires on-face reporting of the net pension liability — this was a major change from prior GASB standards. Wrong (D): GASB 68 uses total pension liability; it is ASC 715 that uses the projected benefit obligation, not the other way around.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Capital asset depreciation in government-wide statements
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$Pinewood City purchased office furniture for $600,000 with an estimated useful life of 10 years and no residual value. The furniture is used by the public works department and was purchased with General Fund resources. Apply GASB 34 to determine how this asset is reported in the government-wide statement of activities after the first full year of use.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No depreciation is reported; the $600,000 is an expenditure in the current period", "A $60,000 depreciation expense is allocated to the public works function", "The $600,000 is reported as a capital outlay expenditure under the public works function", "A $60,000 depreciation expense is reported as a separate line item, not allocated to any function"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34 para. 44-45, capital assets are reported on the government-wide statements and depreciated over their useful lives. Depreciation expense is normally allocated to the function that uses the asset. $600,000 ÷ 10 years = $60,000 per year, allocated to the public works function on the statement of activities. Wrong (A): This describes governmental fund treatment; government-wide statements capitalize and depreciate assets. Wrong (C): Capital outlay expenditures appear in fund-level statements, not in the government-wide statement of activities. Wrong (D): GASB 34 requires depreciation to be reported as a direct expense of the function using the asset, unless it serves multiple functions (then reported as a separate line or allocated).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Assigned vs unassigned fund balance
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$Cranford Borough's city manager, authorized by the governing body, designates $500,000 of the General Fund's remaining fund balance for future technology upgrades. No formal council resolution is passed. Apply GASB 54 to classify this $500,000.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Restricted fund balance", "Committed fund balance", "Assigned fund balance", "Unassigned fund balance"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 54 para. 13, assigned fund balance reflects a government's intended use of resources. The governing body may delegate authority to assign fund balance to another body or official (here, the city manager). Because no formal action of the highest decision-making authority (council resolution) was taken, this does not qualify as committed. Wrong (A): Restricted requires externally enforceable limitations from creditors, grantors, or laws — an internal designation is not a restriction. Wrong (B): Committed requires formal action by the highest level of decision-making authority (e.g., a council resolution), which was not passed here. Wrong (D): The amounts have an expressed intended use via delegation, so they are not unassigned.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — Statement of net position format with deferred items
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$Summit County's government-wide financial data shows: total assets $280 million, deferred outflows of resources $15 million (pension-related), total liabilities $190 million, and deferred inflows of resources $8 million (property tax and pension-related). Evaluate the calculation and significance of net position under the GASB reporting model.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Net position is $105 million, calculated as assets minus liabilities, with deferred items disclosed only in the notes", "Net position is $90 million, calculated as assets minus liabilities minus deferred outflows", "Net position is $82 million, reflecting assets minus liabilities minus deferred outflows plus deferred inflows", "Net position is $97 million, calculated as assets plus deferred outflows minus liabilities minus deferred inflows"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 63 and GASB 65, the statement of net position equation is: Assets + Deferred Outflows of Resources − Liabilities − Deferred Inflows of Resources = Net Position. $280M + $15M − $190M − $8M = $97M. Deferred outflows and inflows are distinct elements reported on the face of the statement, not merely footnote disclosures. Wrong (A): This ignores deferred outflows and deferred inflows, which are separate elements that affect net position under GASB 63. Wrong (B): This incorrectly subtracts deferred outflows; they should be added because they represent consumption of resources applicable to future periods. Wrong (C): This reverses the treatment — deferred outflows are added and deferred inflows are subtracted, not vice versa.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — GASB vs FASB revenue recognition comparison
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$A CPA is transitioning from auditing private companies under FASB standards to auditing a municipal government under GASB standards. Apply the relevant frameworks to determine how governmental fund revenue recognition differs from commercial revenue recognition under ASC 606.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Governmental funds recognize revenue when measurable and available, while ASC 606 uses a five-step model based on satisfying performance obligations", "Both frameworks recognize revenue when earned and realizable, with identical timing criteria", "Governmental funds use the five-step performance obligation model, while ASC 606 uses measurable and available criteria", "Governmental funds recognize revenue only when cash is received, while ASC 606 uses the accrual basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under modified accrual accounting (GASB Codification 1600), governmental fund revenues are recognized when measurable and available (collectible within the current period or soon enough thereafter, typically 60 days). ASC 606 uses a five-step model: identify the contract, identify performance obligations, determine transaction price, allocate to obligations, and recognize when satisfied. These are fundamentally different frameworks. Wrong (B): The frameworks use entirely different recognition criteria — availability versus performance obligation satisfaction. Wrong (C): This reverses which framework applies to which entity type. Wrong (D): Governmental funds use modified accrual, not cash basis — revenues can be recognized before cash receipt if they are available.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Budget reversal at year-end
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$The City of Norwood recorded the following budgetary entry at the start of its fiscal year: Debit Estimated Revenues $20 million, Credit Appropriations $19.5 million, Credit Budgetary Fund Balance $500,000. At the end of the fiscal year, what entry is required under GASB budgetary accounting?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No entry is required; the budgetary accounts remain open permanently", "Debit Appropriations $19.5M, Debit Budgetary Fund Balance $500K, Credit Estimated Revenues $20M", "Debit Appropriations $19.5M, Credit Estimated Revenues $19.5M", "Debit Budgetary Fund Balance $20M, Credit Estimated Revenues $20M"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB budgetary accounting, the budgetary entry recorded at the beginning of the year is reversed at year-end to remove the budgetary accounts from the ledger. The reversing entry is the exact opposite of the original: Debit Appropriations $19.5M, Debit Budgetary Fund Balance $500K, Credit Estimated Revenues $20M. Wrong (A): Budgetary accounts are temporary and must be closed at year-end; they do not carry forward. Wrong (C): This omits the Budgetary Fund Balance component and does not fully reverse the original entry. Wrong (D): This does not properly reverse the original budgetary entry and uses incorrect accounts and amounts.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Fiduciary fund types
(4, 'State and Local Government Reporting', $EXPL$Under GASB standards, which of the following is one of the four types of fiduciary funds?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Capital projects fund", "Debt service fund", "Pension trust fund", "Special revenue fund"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): GASB 84 identifies four fiduciary fund types: pension (and other employee benefit) trust funds, investment trust funds, private-purpose trust funds, and custodial funds. Pension trust funds account for resources held in trust for employee retirement benefits. Wrong (A): Capital projects funds are governmental funds used to account for resources restricted for acquiring or constructing major capital assets. Wrong (B): Debt service funds are governmental funds used to accumulate resources for paying principal and interest on long-term debt. Wrong (D): Special revenue funds are governmental funds for legally restricted revenue sources, not fiduciary funds.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1)
;

